To write a first-person controller script in GDScript and apply it to a KinematicBody-node in Godot Engine 3.x, follow these steps:

1. Create a new script and name it "FirstPersonController.gd".
2. Open the script and add the following code:

```gdscript
extends KinematicBody

var player_speed = 100
var camera_speed = 50

func _ready():
    get_tree().create_collision_shape(get_shape())

func _process(delta):
    var input = Input.get_mouse_position()
    var mouse_delta = input.sub(get_viewport_mouse_pos())

    var forward_vector = Vector3.up.rotated(-math.radians(get_transform().yaw), Vector3.right)
    var right_vector = Vector3.right.rotated(-math.radians(get_transform().yaw - math.pi / 2), Vector3.up)

    var move_vector = forward_vector * mouse_delta.y * camera_speed + right_vector * mouse_delta.x * camera_speed

    move_and_slide(move_vector * player_speed * delta)

    var camera_rotation = Quaternion.look_rotation(Vector3.zero, move_vector, Vector3.up)
    set_transform(get_transform().translated(move_vector * player_speed * delta), camera_rotation)
```

3. Attach the script to the KinematicBody-node in the Inspector.

In Godot Engine 4.x, the equivalent to the KinematicBody-node is the PhysicsBody2D-node with the "Kinematic" flag enabled. To apply the first-person controller script to a PhysicsBody2D-node in Godot Engine 4.x, follow these steps:

1. Create a new script and name it "FirstPersonController.gd".
2. Open the script and add the following code:

```gdscript
extends Node2D

var player_speed = 100
var camera_speed = 50

func _ready():
    get_tree().create_collision_shape(get_shape())

func _process(delta):
    var input = Input.get_mouse_position()
    var mouse_delta = input.sub(get_viewport_mouse_pos())

    var forward_vector = Vector2.up.rotated(-math.radians(get_transform().rotation.yaw), Vector2.right)
    var right_vector = Vector2.right.rotated(-math.radians(get_transform().rotation.yaw - math.pi / 2), Vector2.up)

    var move_vector = forward_vector * mouse_delta.y * camera_speed + right_vector * mouse_delta.x * camera_speed

    physics_body.apply_linear_impulse(move_vector * player_speed * delta, Vector2.zero)

    var camera_rotation = Quaternion.look_rotation(Vector2.zero, move_vector, Vector2.up)
    set_transform(get_transform().translated(move_vector * player_speed * delta), camera_rotation)
```

3. Attach the script to the PhysicsBody2D-node with the "Kinematic" flag enabled in the Inspector.

Note that in Godot Engine 4.x, the script uses Node2D instead of KinematicBody and applies linear impulses instead of move_and_slide(). Also, the rotation is applied to the transform instead of the set_transform() method.

